---

<section
  id="capabilities"
  class="py-12 sm:py-16 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 max-w-7xl mx-auto border-t-2 border-ink/15"
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mb-8">
    <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight uppercase leading-none">
      What OmniBuf does beyond Protobuf.
    </h2>
  </div>

  <div class="border-t-2 border-ink divide-y-2 divide-ink/20">
    <!-- Row 1: Schema-Less Wire Inspection -->
    <div
      id="explicit-wire-types"
      class="scroll-mt-16 py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-center"
    >
      <div class="lg:col-span-6 space-y-2.5 min-w-0">
        <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight leading-snug">
          Self-describing wire types
        </h3>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/85 leading-relaxed max-w-[65ch]">
          Protobuf only has a 3-bit wire type, so strings, byte slices, sub-messages, maps, and
          packed arrays all get lumped into <code>Wire Type 2</code>. Fixed 32-bit and 64-bit
          numbers mix floats with signed and unsigned integers. OmniBuf widens the wire type to 4
          bits and puts element types on arrays and maps. Running the{' '}
          <a href="/tooling" class="doc-link">
            <code>omnibuf</code> CLI
          </a>{' '}
          or calling <code>DecodeBlind</code> on a raw buffer returns the exact numbers, booleans,
          strings, arrays, and maps that were sent. You only need the schema for things that aren't
          sent on the wire, like enum names, default values, or <code>oneof</code> groupings.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-6 space-y-3 font-mono text-sm min-w-0">
        <div class="p-3.5 bg-[#FAF8F2] border-2 border-ink rounded-md overflow-x-auto">
          <!-- prettier-ignore -->
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2 text-xs font-bold text-ink/75 mb-1.5">
            <a href="https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protoscope" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="doc-link">$ protoscope packet.pb</a>
            <span class="font-normal">Protobuf</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-ink/85 leading-snug"><span class="text-[#C23000] font-bold">3:</span> 1                <span class="text-ink/75"># int or bool?</span>
<span class="text-[#C23000] font-bold">9:</span> &#123;<span class="text-ink/75">"\x1a\x03FRA\x20*"</span>&#125; <span class="text-ink/75"># string, bytes, or sub-msg?</span></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="p-3.5 bg-ink text-paper border-2 border-ink rounded-md overflow-x-auto">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2 text-xs font-bold text-acid mb-1.5">
            <span>$ omnibuf -c packet.obuf</span>
            <span class="font-normal text-paper/65">OmniBuf</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-paper/95 leading-snug">&#123;<span class="text-acid font-bold">"field_3":</span> <span class="text-[#FF4F18] font-bold">true</span>, <span class="text-acid font-bold">"field_9":</span> &#123;<span class="text-acid">"field_3":</span> <span class="text-paper font-bold">"FRA"</span>, <span class="text-acid">"field_4":</span> 42&#125;&#125;</pre>
        </div>
        <div class="p-3.5 bg-ink text-paper border-2 border-ink rounded-md overflow-x-auto">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2 text-xs font-bold text-acid mb-1.5">
            <span>$ omnibuf -c packet-field0.obuf</span>
            <span class="font-normal text-acid/85">OmniBuf + Field #0</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-paper/95 leading-snug">&#123;<span class="text-acid font-bold">"active":</span> <span class="text-[#FF4F18] font-bold">true</span>, <span class="text-acid font-bold">"node":</span> &#123;<span class="text-acid">"city":</span> <span class="text-paper font-bold">"FRA"</span>, <span class="text-acid">"asn":</span> 42&#125;&#125;</pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Row 2: Optional Field #0 Inline Field Names -->
    <div
      id="field-0-names"
      class="scroll-mt-16 py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-center"
    >
      <div class="lg:col-span-6 space-y-3 min-w-0">
        <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight leading-snug">
          Optional field names in Field #0
        </h3>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/85 leading-relaxed max-w-[65ch]">
          By default, OmniBuf only writes numeric field IDs to keep packets small. Turning on{' '}
          <code>Field #0</code> (<code>omnibuf.v1.SchemaMeta</code>) writes a field-number-to-name
          map once at the start of the packet so the wire bytes carry their own string field names.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/85 leading-relaxed max-w-[65ch]">
          For arrays and maps of structs, child field names are stored once in{' '}
          <a href="/spec#sec-8-1" class="doc-link">
            <code>SchemaMeta.children</code>
          </a>{' '}
          and inherited by every item in the list. In JSON or MessagePack, an array of 1,000 objects
          repeats the key strings <code>"router_id"</code> and <code>"latency_ns"</code> 1,000
          times. OmniBuf writes those strings once in Field #0 and uses compact field numbers inside
          the elements. Even on our benchmark packet with just 4 <code>Neighbor</code> items, a
          self-describing OmniBuf packet (630 B) is smaller than MessagePack (656 B) and JSON (803
          B).
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-6 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm min-w-0">
        <div class="os-window overflow-hidden">
          <div class="os-titlebar bg-[#E5E0D5]">
            <span class="font-bold uppercase text-ink text-xs">
              1,000-Item Array: Repeated Keys vs. Field #0 Inheritance
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-ink text-paper p-4 space-y-3">
            <div class="bg-[#1F1F1F] p-3.5 rounded border border-paper/15">
              <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2 text-xs mb-2">
                <span class="uppercase font-bold text-[#FF4F18]">JSON &amp; MessagePack</span>
                <span class="text-paper/65">keys repeated 1,000x</span>
              </div>
              <pre class="leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>"neighbors": [
  &#123;<span class="text-[#FF4F18]">"router_id"</span>: "fra-1", <span class="text-[#FF4F18]">"latency_ns"</span>: 420&#125;,
  &#123;<span class="text-[#FF4F18]">"router_id"</span>: "ams-2", <span class="text-[#FF4F18]">"latency_ns"</span>: 890&#125;,
  ...
]</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-[#1F1F1F] p-3.5 rounded border border-acid/45 space-y-2.5">
              <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2 text-xs">
                <span class="uppercase font-bold text-acid">OmniBuf + Field #0</span>
                <span class="text-acid/85">names written once</span>
              </div>
              <pre class="leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code><span class="text-paper/65">// Field #0: name table (once per packet)</span>
<span class="text-acid">10</span>   = "neighbors"
<span class="text-acid">10.1</span> = "router_id"
<span class="text-acid">10.2</span> = "latency_ns"

<span class="text-paper/65">// Field #10: 1,000 items (numeric IDs only)</span>
&#123;<span class="text-acid">1</span>: "fra-1", <span class="text-acid">2</span>: 420&#125;
&#123;<span class="text-acid">1</span>: "ams-2", <span class="text-acid">2</span>: 890&#125;
...</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
